
  ---

  [1mThree approaches[0m

  The real architectural question is [1mwhether a notification is a stored thing or a computed thing[0m. Everything else
  follows from that.

  [1mA. Derived / stateless projection.[0m No notification records at all. The notification list is a pure function of [38;5;153m(tasks,
[39m  [38;5;153mnow)[39m — filter for due and overdue, render. Only persisted extra state is a per-task dismissal marker.
  [3mWins when[0m you want the fewest moving parts. No dedup logic, no drift, deleting a task can't orphan a notification.
  [3mCosts:[0m no history, and — the real problem — a pure projection has no memory, so it can't tell "this is newly overdue"
  from "this has been overdue for three days." OS toasts would re-fire on every reload.
  [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mB. Materialized store / event log.[0m A scheduler ticks, detects transitions ([38;5;153mupcoming → due → overdue[39m), and appends
  persisted records: [38;5;153m{id, taskId, kind, createdAt, readAt}[39m. The UI renders that list.
  [3mWins when[0m you want real history and an obvious extension point — the watched-task producer you deferred drops straight
  in as a second writer.
  [3mCosts:[0m idempotency is the entire design problem. Every tick, every reload, every clock jump must not re-raise what's
  already raised, so you need a stable identity and a watermark anyway. Plus orphan handling: delete a task and its
  notifications outlive it.
  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Derived list + persisted delivery ledger.[0m The visible list is computed from [38;5;153m(tasks, now)[39m as in A. Alongside it, a
  small persisted ledger of keys — [38;5;153m(taskId, kind)[39m → [38;5;153m{deliveredAt, readAt}[39m — whose only jobs are suppressing duplicate OS
  toasts and tracking read state.
  [3mWins when[0m you want A's freedom from drift plus B's correct once-only delivery. Deleting or completing a task removes
  its notification for free, because the list was never stored. Unread counts and toast-once both come from the ledger.
  The ledger is a keyed set, not a parallel record list, so there's much less to keep in sync.
  [3mCosts:[0m no history for deleted or completed tasks — once a task is gone, its notification is gone. Two concepts instead
  of one.
  [3mComplexity: moderate, but less than B.[0m

